            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner (William C. Ingram), petitioner argued that \u201cbrandish\u201d should be defined as displaying a firearm to intimidate, includes a mens\u2011rea element, and therefore must be proved as an element of the offense rather than treated merely as a sentencing enhancement."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner (William C. Ingram)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2dba323396db574abee852ed74d06579ba01a4084acf77cdae704daf7cd976da",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2dba323396db-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 5,
              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner (William C. Ingram), petitioner also contended that the statutory increase of the mandatory minimum for brandishing from five to seven years represents a 40\u202f% rise, and that the penalty for discharging a firearm doubles, highlighting the severity of the enhanced sentencing."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondent",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "2dba323396db574abee852ed74d06579ba01a4084acf77cdae704daf7cd976da",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-2dba323396db-0001-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 33,
              "start": 33
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Respondent argued that before the amendment to 18\u202fU.S.C.\u202f\u00a7\u202f924(c) judges had no sentencing discretion, and that Congress changed the law to treat the conduct as a more serious crime."
      },
